    "created_at": "2022-11-17T14:29:37Z",
    "updated_at": "2022-11-17T14:30:40Z",
    "body": "Different idea: We stick with (1) the implicit `open` attribute, when the content is targeted. To allow developers more control we introduce two more attributes:\r\n\r\n* `dialog` and `details` can have the `disabled` attribute. This disables opening them at all. The DOM reflects the attribute in a new property `disabled`.\r\n* `dialog` can have a new attribute `modal`. This forces the dialog to open modally, if it opens. As long as the dialog is not open, this attribute has no effect.\r\n\r\nThis gives developers the option to prevent automatic opening with setting the `disabled` attribute in the initial HTML payload, until JS can take over. With `modal` the way a `dialog` opens can be controlled from markup, too.\r\n\r\n```html\r\n<details>\r\n  <summary>opens when linked into</summary>\r\n  <p id=\"link-here\">Some content</p>\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n<details disabled>\r\n  <summary>does <strong>not</strong> open when linked into</summary>\r\n  <p id=\"link-here\">Some content</p>\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n<dialog modal>\r\n  <p id=\"in-modal-dialog\">will open modally when linked into.</p>\r\n</dialog>\r\n```",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
